--- Test cases ---

Four test cases are provided in $FEBIO_ROOT/pace-tests/:

  tu01.feb        Tensile test: 20x5x5 hex block (Mooney-Rivlin), prescribed
                  x-displacement of 1 unit at t=1. Static analysis, 10 steps.

  tu01_heavy.feb  Enlarged version of tu01 for parallelization benchmarking.

  tu02.feb        Torsion test: same block attached to a rigid body with an
                  x-rotation of 3.14 radians at t=1. Static analysis, 10 steps.

  tu03.feb        Compression test: quarter-symmetry billet compressed by a
                  rigid wall with z-displacement of 0.4 units at t=1.
                  Note: AUTO STEPPER (adaptive time stepping) is expected
                  behavior for this problem, not a failure.

--- Multi-threading (OpenMP) ---

FEBio is a single-process OpenMP application. It automatically uses all
CPUs allocated by Slurm via SLURM_CPUS_PER_TASK — no srun and no
OMP_NUM_THREADS are needed.

In a batch script:
   #SBATCH --nodes=1
   #SBATCH --cpus-per-task=8

   module load febio/4.12
   febio4 -i model.feb -o model.log

Or interactively:
   $ salloc -N1 --cpus-per-task=6
   $ febio4 -i model.feb -o model.log

Important: do NOT launch FEBio with srun. Using srun will spawn one
independent FEBio process per task slot rather than one multi-threaded
process, which is incorrect.

Parallelization benchmark using tu01_heavy.feb on Phoenix dev cluster:
   1 CPU  ->  Elapsed time : 0:00:19
   6 CPUs ->  Elapsed time : 0:00:09

--- GUI (FEBio Studio 3.1) ---

FEBio Studio requires a graphical session. Use the Open OnDemand
Interactive Desktop at https://ondemand.pace.gatech.edu, then run:

   module load febio/4.12
   FEBioStudio

Note: FEBio Studio 3.1 was built on Ubuntu 22.04 and requires GLIBC_2.35.
Phoenix nodes run RHEL9 with GLIBC_2.34. The GUI is currently unavailable
on Phoenix. The command-line solver (febio4) is unaffected. A containerized
version of FEBio Studio is planned as a follow-up.

